      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          This page is not a list of this year's deals — those change by the hour and a list
          would be out of date before you read it. It is the method for judging any treadmill
          deal you see between mid-November and Cyber Monday, so you can tell a genuine
          reduction from a big red percentage in a few minutes.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the figures below.</strong> Discount ranges, delivery
          costs and retailer policies here are typical patterns, not quotes. They vary by brand,
          retailer and year, and the only figures that count are the ones on the order page in
          front of you — including the delivery tier and the return terms.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Are Treadmill Black Friday Deals Real?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#before-the-sale">Do the Work Before the Sale Starts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#judge-the-price">Judge the Price Against What It Actually Sold For</a></li>
            <li><a href="#msrp-inflation">MSRP Inflation and the "Was" Price</a></li>
            <li><a href="#subscriptions-as-discount">Bundled Subscriptions Counted as Savings</a></li>
            <li><a href="#other-bundles">Free Mats, Accessories and Extended Warranties</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivery-fees">Delivery Fees and the Delivered Price</a></li>
            <li><a href="#which-tiers">Which Treadmill Tiers Discount Most</a></li>
            <li><a href="#deal-formats">Doorbusters, Lightning Deals and Marketplace Sellers</a></li>
            <li><a href="#returns">Returns, Price Adjustments and Holiday Policies</a></li>
            <li><a href="#january">January as the Alternative</a></li>
            <li><a href="#checklist">A Black Friday Treadmill Checklist</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Are Treadmill Black Friday Deals Real?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Often, yes.</strong> For treadmills, late November is reliably one of the
            strongest buying windows of the year, because retailers would rather move stock at a
            reduced margin before the January rush than store it for six more weeks. Genuine
            reductions on mainstream home treadmills are common.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>But the size of the discount is frequently overstated.</strong> The headline
            percentage is usually measured against a list price, and on fitness equipment the list
            price is often a number the machine rarely sells at. A genuine treadmill discount in a
            strong window typically runs somewhere around 15 to 30% off the normal selling price;
            claims of half off or more are almost always measured against a reference price that
            nobody pays.
          </p>
          <p>
            The test is simple to state and takes a little preparation: compare the delivered
            price — machine, delivery tier and any required extras — against what the same machine
            sold for in the weeks before the sale, at more than one retailer. If that number is
            meaningfully lower, it is a deal. If the only thing that changed is the "was" price,
            it is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy</a> guide covers why prices move across the whole year; this page is about the sale
            itself. For the specific machines worth watching this year, with what each normally
            sells for and the price we would wait for, see <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill deals 2026</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-the-sale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Do the Work Before the Sale Starts</h2>
<p>
            Most bad Black Friday purchases are decided in the moment. A countdown timer and a large
            percentage are designed to make you choose a machine during the sale, which is the worst
            time to choose one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose two or three acceptable machines by early November.</strong> Work out
            what you need first: deck length for your stride, motor rating for your weight and pace,
            folding or not, and the space you have. Our <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying guide</a> and <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a> cover those decisions. A shortlist of more than one means you can buy whichever falls
            furthest instead of chasing one model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Record the normal selling price.</strong> Note what each shortlisted machine
            actually sells for in October and early November, at more than one retailer, with the
            delivery tier you would choose. A price-tracking tool or a dated screenshot both work.
            This is the baseline every deal is measured against. The <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/#tracking" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tracking section</a> of our timing guide covers the mechanics.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set your ceiling.</strong> Decide in advance what you will pay for each machine
            delivered. If a deal meets it, buy; if it does not, you have lost nothing by waiting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="judge-the-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Judge the Price Against What It Actually Sold For</h2>
<p>
            The only comparison that matters is between the price you can pay now and the price you
            could have paid recently. Everything on a deal page — the strikethrough, the percentage,
            the "you save" figure — is calculated from something else. Our <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT review</a> works through a real reference-versus-sale-price example.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use price history, not the reference price.</strong> If you did not record a
            baseline yourself, a price history tool for the retailer can show how the price moved
            over recent months. Look at the typical price, not the single highest point. A machine
            that sold most of the year around one figure and is now meaningfully below it is on a
            real reduction, whatever the badge says.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the same machine at other sellers on the same day.</strong> Genuine
            promotions on mainstream models tend to move the price at several retailers at once. A
            discount that exists at only one seller and is measured from a list price the others do
            not use is usually a presentation, not a reduction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Make sure it is the same machine.</strong> Model numbers on treadmills change
            with model years and retailer-specific variants, and two listings with similar names
            can differ in motor, deck, console or warranty. Compare the specifications line by
            line, not the product name.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Remember the whole week counts.</strong> Treadmill reductions frequently begin
            before the day itself and continue to <a href="/cyber-monday-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cyber Monday</a> and beyond. Missing a single day
            rarely means missing the sale.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="msrp-inflation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>MSRP Inflation and the "Was" Price</h2>
<p>
            Reference pricing is a long-standing problem in fitness equipment, and the sale period
            is when it is most visible.
          </p>
          <p>
            The pattern is familiar: a machine shown as "was" a high figure, "now" a much lower one,
            with a percentage badge between them. The "was" figure may be the manufacturer's
            suggested retail price, a "compare at" price, or simply a price the item was listed at
            briefly. If the machine has been selling well below it for months, the discount is
            arithmetic against a number nobody paid.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some warning signs are worth knowing:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>A percentage far larger than the 15 to 30% that genuine treadmill reductions typically reach.</li>
            <li>A "compare at" or "list" price that does not appear at any other retailer.</li>
            <li>A price that rose in the weeks before the sale, so the reduction lands back where it started.</li>
            <li>A "limited time" price that seems to be permanently limited.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this means the machine is a bad buy at the sale price. It means the percentage is
            not information. Judge the delivered price against your baseline and against what the
            machine is worth for your use. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what a treadmill costs</a> gives typical prices by tier, which is a useful sense check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscriptions-as-discount" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Bundled Subscriptions Counted as Savings</h2>
<p>
            Connected treadmills are often promoted with a period of membership included, and some
            deal pages count the retail value of that membership in the advertised saving. It is
            worth separating the two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Included membership is only worth what you would have paid anyway.</strong> If you
            were going to subscribe, months of included membership are a real saving at the rate you
            would have paid. If you were not, they are worth nothing to you — and an included period
            that renews automatically into a paid plan can become a cost.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Some machines require or strongly assume a membership.</strong> On those, the
            membership is part of the cost of ownership, and an included period simply delays when
            you start paying it. Price the machine over the years you expect to own it, not over the
            first one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Watch the renewal terms.</strong> Note when the included period ends and what it
            renews into. Our <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> works through the long-run cost of a membership, and <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> covers machines where this question does not arise.
          </p>
          <p>
            A useful habit: compare the deal with the bundled membership removed. If the machine
            price on its own is a good reduction, the membership is a bonus. If the saving only
            looks large because the membership is counted in it, it is not much of a deal on the
            treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-bundles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Free Mats, Accessories and Extended Warranties</h2>
<p>
            Sale-period bundles often include physical extras, and the same rule applies: count them
            at what you would have spent, not at the retail value printed on the deal.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mats and accessories.</strong> A mat is useful on most floors, and if you would
            have bought one anyway, an included one is worth having. Our guide to whether <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">you need a treadmill mat</a> covers when it matters. Bundled heart rate straps, lubricant and cleaning kits are
            useful but low value, and rarely a reason to choose one deal over another.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Extended warranties and protection plans.</strong> These are often offered at
            checkout and sometimes bundled into deals. Read what the manufacturer's warranty
            already covers first, because on many treadmills the frame and motor terms are long
            and the plan mostly duplicates them. Where a plan adds labour or parts cover beyond the
            standard terms, it may be worth considering; compare the terms, not the label. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a> covers what the standard terms usually include.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Upgraded delivery or assembly.</strong> This is the bundle most often worth more
            than it looks, because it is a cost you would genuinely otherwise pay. A slightly higher
            headline price with room-of-choice delivery included can beat a lower one with kerbside
            only. The next section covers it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-fees" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery Fees and the Delivered Price</h2>
<p>
            Comparing headline prices during a sale misses the cost that varies most between
            retailers. Delivery tiers differ in what they include — from kerbside drop-off to
            room-of-choice placement and assembly — and upgrades can add a meaningful sum. Our <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/#delivery-costs" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing guide</a> sets out typical costs, and <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy</a> covers what each channel usually includes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Black Friday adds three specific wrinkles.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>"Free shipping" usually means kerbside.</strong> A sale price with free shipping
            is often a crate at the end of your driveway. If the machine has to go upstairs or into a
            basement, price that before comparing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Freight is busier in late November and December.</strong> Delivery windows for
            large items can stretch, and scheduled freight delivery may be weeks out. If the
            treadmill is a gift or needs to arrive by a date, check the delivery estimate on the
            order page rather than assuming.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Some deals exclude upgraded delivery or assembly.</strong> Read whether the sale
            price applies with every delivery tier, and whether assembly is available at all for
            that order.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever the price, measure the route from the door to the room before you order. A
            discount does not help if the box will not fit round the stairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Treadmill Tiers Discount Most</h2>
<p>
            Discounting is not even across the market. As general patterns rather than predictions
            for any given year:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads and compact machines</strong> are promoted heavily, especially on
            marketplaces, but many of them are discounted frequently throughout the year. A Black
            Friday price may not be unusual. Price history is especially important here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Budget folding treadmills</strong> attract the largest headline percentages, and
            also the most inflated reference prices. Real reductions do happen, but judge them
            against the selling price, and make sure the machine is adequate for your use; our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$500 guide</a> covers what that tier can and cannot do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mainstream mid-range home treadmills</strong> from the major brands are often where
            the most useful genuine reductions appear, typically within that 15 to 30% band on the
            normal selling price in a strong year. This is also where outgoing model years are most
            often cleared.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Premium and specialist machines</strong> tend to see smaller cuts to the headline
            price. Promotions here more often take the form of included delivery, assembly,
            accessories or membership, which can still be worth real money.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial and dealer-sold machines</strong> may not be part of mainstream sales
            at all. Some dealers run their own promotions or have room to negotiate, particularly
            on floor models and outgoing stock; ask.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deal-formats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Doorbusters, Lightning Deals and Marketplace Sellers</h2>
<p>
            The format of a deal matters almost as much as the price, because some formats limit what
            you can check.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Time-limited and limited-quantity deals</strong> are built to compress your
            decision. They are fine if the machine is already on your shortlist and the price beats
            your baseline. They are a poor way to discover a machine. If a countdown is the main
            reason you are considering it, let it run out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Marketplace listings</strong> can come from the manufacturer, an authorised
            retailer, or an unrelated third-party seller. That matters for warranty, because some
            manufacturers limit warranty support to purchases from authorised sellers, and for
            returns, which may be handled by the seller rather than the marketplace. Check who the
            seller is before buying a large item.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Open-box, refurbished and floor models</strong> often appear in sale periods.
            They can be excellent value, but the warranty terms may differ from a new unit, so ask
            what is covered and for how long. Our guide to <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills</a> covers what to ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price matching</strong> exists at some retailers and not others, and sale periods
            are often excluded. If you want to rely on it, read the current policy rather than
            assuming.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Returns, Price Adjustments and Holiday Policies</h2>
<p>
            The return policy on a treadmill is worth reading before the sale, not after delivery.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Large items often have their own rules.</strong> Retailers that offer generous or
            extended holiday returns on most products sometimes treat fitness equipment and
            freight-delivered items differently. There may be a shorter window, a requirement for <a href="/how-to-pack-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>original packaging</a>, a restocking fee, or a charge for return freight. Some return
            processes require the item to be disassembled and boxed, which on a treadmill is a
            substantial job.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly can affect returnability.</strong> Some policies distinguish between
            unopened, opened and assembled items. Check whether <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>professional assembly</a> changes what
            happens if you want to return it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price adjustments are not universal.</strong> If the price falls further after
            you buy, some retailers will refund the difference within a set period; many will not,
            and sale periods are commonly excluded. Know which applies before deciding whether to
            buy on the Tuesday or wait for Cyber Monday.
          </p>
          <p>
            Save the order confirmation, the product page as it appeared, and the policy that applied
            on the day. If anything goes wrong, those are what you will need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="january" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>January as the Alternative</h2>
<p>
            If Black Friday passes without a deal that meets your ceiling, or you simply are not ready,
            January is the obvious second chance — with one important qualification.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid the first two weeks.</strong> Late December through early January is when
            demand peaks, and retailers have little reason to discount. Promotional activity in that
            window is often measured against inflated list prices.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The second half of January is better.</strong> Once the new-year rush has passed,
            retailers who stocked heavily for it start competing again. It is generally not as strong
            as late November, but it is a good window, and it has the advantage of coming after you
            have had more time to decide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Model-year clearance can beat both.</strong> Outgoing models that are mechanically
            near-identical to their replacements are cleared at various points in the year, and they
            are often the best value in the category. Our <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing guide</a> covers the full calendar, including the windows outside November and January.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you are ready to start now, waiting six weeks to save a modest sum is only worth it
            if you will actually start in six weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Black Friday Treadmill Checklist</h2>
<p>
            Run every deal through these before you click buy.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Is it on my shortlist?</strong> If not, it is not a deal for you, whatever the percentage.</li>
            <li><strong>Is it the same model?</strong> Compare model numbers and specifications, not names.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the price below my recorded baseline?</strong> Against what it actually sold for, not the "was" price.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the delivered price competitive?</strong> Include delivery tier, assembly and any required extras.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the saving real without the bundle?</strong> Remove membership and accessories you would not have bought.</li>
            <li><strong>Who is the seller?</strong> Manufacturer, authorised retailer or third party, and what that means for warranty.</li>
            <li><strong>What are the return terms for this item?</strong> Window, restocking and freight charges, packaging, assembly.</li>
            <li><strong>Will it arrive when I need it?</strong> Check the delivery estimate on the order page.</li>
            <li><strong>Will it fit?</strong> Room, ceiling height for running and the route from the door.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If a deal passes all nine, buy it and stop looking. The most common Black Friday regret
            is not paying slightly too much; it is spending a week chasing a slightly lower price and
            ending up with a machine you had not chosen. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much to spend</a> helps set the ceiling in the first place.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Black Friday Treadmill Deals</h2>
          <p>Nearly every bad sale purchase comes from letting the deal choose the machine, or from comparing the wrong price.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing the machine during the sale</h3>
              <p>A countdown and a large percentage push you to pick a treadmill in minutes. Decide two or three acceptable machines before November, based on your space, weight and pace, and only consider deals on those. A heavily discounted wrong machine is not a saving.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the percentage badge</h3>
              <p>The advertised saving is calculated from a reference price that the machine may rarely sell at. Compare against what it actually sold for in the weeks before the sale, at more than one retailer. Genuine reductions typically run around 15 to 30% of the normal selling price.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring delivery and returns</h3>
              <p>Free shipping often means kerbside, and holiday return extensions may not cover freight-delivered fitness equipment. Price the delivery tier you need and read the return terms for this item, including restocking and return freight, before treating the headline price as the cost.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Counting bundled extras at retail value</h3>
              <p>Included membership, accessories and protection plans are only worth what you would have spent on them. Remove anything you would not have bought and see whether the treadmill itself is discounted. If the saving disappears, the deal was mostly packaging.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Black Friday is usually a strong time to buy a treadmill</strong>,
            but the deal is the delivered price against what the machine actually sold for — not
            the percentage on the badge. Genuine reductions typically land around 15 to 30% off the
            normal selling price.
          </p>
          <p>
            Shortlist two or three machines, record their prices in October, and buy whichever meets
            your ceiling once delivery, bundles and return terms are counted. If nothing does, <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">the second half of January</a> is the next good window.
          </p>
        </div>

